  "summary": "One square cloth knots into bag, bottle-carrier and gift box — wrapping as reusable design.",
  "description": "Furoshiki — literally \"bath spread\" — began as Edo-period cloths for bundling clothes at public baths and became Japan's universal carrier: a single hemmed square (silk, cotton, or nylon today) knotted into backpacks, two-bottle totes, fruit cradles and gift wrap, its pattern part of the present. Ministry-of-environment campaigns revived furoshiki in 2006 as plastic-bag alternatives, and design studios now print everything from chiyogami motifs to contemporary art on the square. The knots (honmusubi, uzura-musubi) carry the craft.",
